     1	//! `PluginObjectBlob` — the sealed, self-describing projection of one
     2	//! `PluginSchema` into one runtime identity (whitepaper "Target Data Model").
     3	//!
     4	//! The blob couples the four identities that must not drift:
     5	//! schema identity (canonical JSON + sha256), D-Bus identity, gRPC identity,
     6	//! and the reflection descriptor identity. It is deliberately redundant at the
     7	//! blob boundary: schema stays authoritative, but the blob is self-describing
     8	//! enough that reflection, GUI, compliance, and dispatch reason about the same
     9	//! object without reassembling identity from scattered files.
    10	//!
    11	//! ## Sealed byte format (zero-copy)
    12	//!
    13	//! ```text
    14	//! offset  size  content
    15	//! 0       8     magic "OPBLOB01"
    16	//! 8       2     format version (u16 LE) = 1
    17	//! 10      2     section count (u16 LE)
    18	//! 12      4     reserved
    19	//! 16      32    sha256 of the SCHEMA_JSON section (the schema hash)
    20	//! 48      16    reserved
    21	//! 64      24*n  section table: tag u32, reserved u32, offset u64, len u64
    22	//! ...           8-byte-aligned section payloads
    23	//! ```
    24	//!
    25	//! Sections: 1 = canonical `PluginSchema` JSON, 2 = `BlobManifest` JSON,
    26	//! 3 = protobuf `FileDescriptorSet`, 4 = compliance/extra metadata JSON.
    27	//!
    28	//! `BlobRef` verifies magic + hash once, then hands out borrowed slices —
    29	//! reads never copy or re-parse the payload. Sealed bytes are fully
    30	//! deterministic for the same schema (sorted maps, no timestamps), so the
    31	//! schema hash doubles as the artifact identity in the filename:
    32	//! `<plugin_id>.<hash16>.blob`.
